# Layering — import rules

Dependency direction is strictly one-way:

```
app  →  hunt  →  core
```

## Hard rules

- `ro_bot/core/**` MUST NOT import from `ro_bot.hunt` or `ro_bot.app`.
- `ro_bot/hunt/**` MUST NOT import from `ro_bot.app`.
- `ro_bot/hunt/**` MUST NOT read config files directly (configuration
  arrives as `HuntConfig`, not as a JSON dict).
- `ro_bot/core/**` MUST NOT import `argparse` or touch any
  top-level profile / config concept.

## Examples

```python
# ❌ BAD — core reaching up into hunt
# src/ro_bot/core/window.py
from ro_bot.hunt.config import HuntConfig

# ❌ BAD — hunt loading its own config
# src/ro_bot/hunt/controller.py
import json
from pathlib import Path
data = json.loads(Path("config.json").read_text())

# ✅ GOOD — hunt takes already-hydrated dataclass
# src/ro_bot/hunt/controller.py
from ro_bot.hunt.config import HuntConfig

# ✅ GOOD — app wires hunt with core primitives
# src/ro_bot/app/session.py
from ro_bot.core.hid.arduino import ArduinoHidBridge
from ro_bot.hunt.controller import HuntController
```

## Cross-cutting modules

`logging` from stdlib is allowed everywhere. `ro_bot.core.logging_setup`
may be called only from `app` / `cli` — not from inside `core` or
`hunt` module bodies (don't configure logging at import time).
